+section('overview', 'Product overview', 'info-circle')
  p.
    #[strong TestHub] is a small, self-hosted job network. It lets CI/CD pipelines and individual
    developers run firmware and software tests on #[strong real hardware] or #[strong emulators]
    that sit in a private lab behind a firewall. Nothing in the lab has to be reachable from outside.

  .row.g-3.mb-3
    .col-md-4
      .card.h-100
        .card-body
          h3.h6.card-title
            i.bi.bi-hdd-network.me-1
            | Coordinator
          p.card-text.small.mb-0.
            This service. It holds the job queue and the resource registry, and runs the scheduler and
            the heartbeat monitor. It stores logs and artifacts in SQLite plus a local directory,
            and serves this dashboard. It's the only thing that needs a public HTTPS port.
    .col-md-4
      .card.h-100
        .card-body
          h3.h6.card-title
            i.bi.bi-terminal.me-1
            | Agent (#[code thub])
          p.card-text.small.mb-0.
            A CLI, and the only way to create jobs. The same #[code thub run] command works in a
            GitHub Actions step and on a developer's laptop, so a CI failure can be reproduced exactly.
    .col-md-4
      .card.h-100
        .card-body
          h3.h6.card-title
            i.bi.bi-motherboard.me-1
            | Client (#[code thub-client])
          p.card-text.small.mb-0.
            A daemon (a systemd service) on a lab machine. Each instance owns one DUT slot. It connects
            #[em outbound] to the Coordinator, picks up jobs, runs them and streams logs back.
            Type #[strong HW] drives a physical board; type #[strong SW] runs a DUT emulator in Docker.

  h3.h6 How a job flows
  ol.small
    li #[code thub run …] submits a job spec (type, labels, command, inputs) with an agent token.
    li The Coordinator queues it (#[code QUEUED]) and the scheduler assigns it to an idle Client whose type, labels and group match.
    li The Client accepts it, clones #[code --git-repo], downloads #[code --download-file] files and prepares the DUT (#[code PREPARING]).
    li It runs #[code --command] with #[code sh -c], and output streams live to the Agent and the dashboard (#[code RUNNING]).
    li The exit code is the verdict: #[code 0] = #[code PASSED], anything else = #[code FAILED]. JUnit files and artifacts are uploaded.
    li The Agent exits with the verdict code, so a CI step passes or fails with it.

  h3.h6 Key design rules
  ul.small
    li #[strong Clients only connect outbound] over HTTPS: registration, heartbeat, long-poll, logs, results. The lab firewall allows no inbound traffic.
    li #[strong The Agent is the only way to create jobs.] CI and manual runs are identical.
    li #[strong The Coordinator is the single source of truth.] It assigns a job inside a single SQLite transaction, so a job can never be assigned twice.
    li #[strong A job is a command, not a script upload.] The Client runs your #[code --command] as its service user, never as root. Issue agent tokens only to trusted pipelines and people.

  h3.h6 Resource and job states
  .row.g-3.small
    .col-md-6
      table.table.table-sm.mb-0
        thead
          tr
            th Resource
            th Meaning
        tbody
          tr
            td: code REGISTERED
            td Just registered, no heartbeat yet
          tr
            td: code IDLE
            td Online and free: schedulable
          tr
            td: code BUSY
            td Running a job (#[code ci] / #[code cli]) or locked by hand (#[code local])
          tr
            td: code OUT_OF_SERVICE
            td 3 heartbeats missed
          tr
            td: code MAINTENANCE
            td Disabled by an admin
    .col-md-6
      table.table.table-sm.mb-0
        thead
          tr
            th Job
            th Meaning
        tbody
          tr
            td: code QUEUED → ASSIGNED
            td Waiting for, then bound to, a Client
          tr
            td: code PREPARING → RUNNING
            td Inputs and DUT being prepared, then the command runs
          tr
            td: code PASSED / FAILED
            td The command's exit code: 0, or non-zero
          tr
            td: code ERROR / TIMEOUT
            td Infrastructure problem, or #[code --timeout] exceeded
          tr
            td: code CANCELED / LOST
            td Canceled, or the Client went offline mid-job

+section('use-cases', 'Use cases', 'lightbulb')
  -
    const useCases = [
      { icon: 'cpu', title: 'Hardware-in-the-loop CI', text: 'Every push builds firmware in the cloud, then a GitHub Actions test job runs `thub run --type hw --wait`: a lab Client flashes a real board through ST-Link, captures its UART, runs the tests and fails the pipeline on a non-zero exit.' },
      { icon: 'pc-display', title: 'Emulator (SW) test farms', text: 'SW Clients on real or virtual Linux machines run each job’s DUT image (QEMU, Renode, a simulator) in a sandboxed Docker container. The tests talk to it at $THUB_DUT_HOST. Scale out by adding VMs.' },
      { icon: 'person-workspace', title: 'Remote access for developers', text: 'Developers reproduce a CI failure on the exact bench it happened on (`--client lab-hw-01`) from home, with the same command CI ran, and stream the board’s console live.' },
      { icon: 'lock', title: 'Shared benches without collisions', text: 'The scheduler hands out one job per bench. An engineer who needs a board by hand runs `thub-client lock` so nothing is scheduled there until `unlock`.' },
      { icon: 'collection', title: 'Dedicated pools', text: 'Groups and labels (`board:nucleo-f401re`, `uart`) route jobs to the right hardware, e.g. a nightly pool separate from the PR pool.' },
      { icon: 'box-seam', title: 'Any command-line test suite', text: 'Anything that runs from a shell and reports through its exit code: pytest, ctest, Robot Framework, a Dockerized test runner, a flashing and smoke-test script.' }
    ]
  .row.g-3
    each uc in useCases
      .col-md-6
        .d-flex.gap-3
          i.bi.fs-4.text-primary(class=`bi-${uc.icon}`)
          div
            h3.h6.mb-1= uc.title
            p.small.text-body-secondary.mb-0!= uc.text.replace(/`([^`]+)`/g, '<code>$1</code>')
